.TH FLASHKIT-MD 1 "November 2026" "FlashKitLinux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
flashkit-md \- read, write, and inspect Mega Drive / 32X cartridges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flashkit-md
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ ROM ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flashkit-md
uses the FlashKit MD serial protocol to inspect a cartridge, read its ROM, or write a ROM image. The FT232 adapter is detected by USB ID 0403:6001 unless a serial port is specified.
.PP
Writing first checks FlashKit and CFI communication. The bundled protocol backend verifies the write on the active connection in a worker process, which exits to close the serial handle. The tool then reconnects and compares a second readback against the exact prepared image. When auto-detected, the adapter is rediscovered by USB ID after reconnect; an explicit --device path is reused. Reconnection retries for up to 20 seconds and reports an error if multiple FT232 adapters are present. The reconnect does not electrically reset or power-cycle the programmer; no documented hardware-reset command is available for this device.
.PP
Flash chip command compatibility depends on the chip family. Successful CFI detection alone does not guarantee that a chip supports the write sequence. The detected addressable ROM size is not a measurement of physical flash capacity. The default software write limit is 4 MiB. The reference C# GUI clamps writes at 4 MiB; raising this tool's limit does not establish that an AliExpress clone can safely store or map a larger image. Larger images require verified flash capacity and compatible mapper support.
Some recognized cartridge metadata signatures trigger mapper-specific bank selection and timestamp preparation before writing; the prepared image is used for both verification passes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -r, --read\ FILE
Read the cartridge ROM into FILE. The file is overwritten only after confirmation; use --yes to skip the prompt. The size is taken from the cartridge header and limited to 32 MiB.
.TP
.BI -w, --write\ FILE
Write FILE to the cartridge, then verify it on the active connection and again after reconnecting.
.TP
.BI -c, --check
Check FlashKit and cartridge flash communication. This is the default when no operation is selected.
.TP
.B -l, --layout
Report the ROM name, header-declared ROM size, detected addressable ROM size, and detected RAM size. It does not report the physical flash chip capacity.
.TP
.BI -d, --device\ PORT
Use PORT as the serial device, for example /dev/ttyUSB0. The default is to detect a single FT232 device by USB ID. --port is accepted as an alias.
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Show reconnect attempts and device diagnostics.
.TP
.B -y, --yes
Skip the write confirmation and allow overwriting a read output file.
.TP
.BI --max-size\ BYTES
Set the software limit for writes. The default is 4194304 (4 MiB); 0 disables this limit. This does not detect or expand physical cartridge capacity.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show command-line help and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the attached cartridge:
.PP
.nf
.RS
flashkit-md --check
.RE
.fi
.PP
Show detected ROM and RAM measurements:
.PP
.nf
.RS
flashkit-md --layout
.RE
.fi
.PP
Write and reconnect-verify a ROM:
.PP
.nf
.RS
flashkit-md --write game.bin
.RE
.fi
.PP
Read a ROM from an explicitly selected port:
.PP
.nf
.RS
flashkit-md --device /dev/ttyUSB0 --read dump.bin
.RE
.fi
.PP
The positional form remains a shorthand for --write:
.PP
.nf
.RS
flashkit-md game.bin
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Requested operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The operation was cancelled.
.TP
.B 2
Input, device, flash, or verification error.
